      "corneringStiffnessN": 8000,
      "toeOutDeg": 2.0,
      "toeOutDegSource": "Standard 2 degrees of toe-out on every taildragger's mains (Ty 2026-11-10: \"Use 2 degrees toe out on all taildraggers standard unless I adjust based on feel\"). A standing slip angle outboard on each wheel: level the pair cancel, and when one is loaded more its outboard side force is the larger, pushing the aeroplane back toward the light side - \"The loaded tire tries to unload back to the other\". The sign comes from each wheel's own y, so one value means outboard on both (LandingGear.ToeRad). Live-tunable in OPTIONS while flying.",
      "tire": "legacy-aircam-main",
      "tirePressurePsi": 16.1
    },
    {
      "pos": [
        0.513,
        1.05,
        1.25
      ],
      "springN": 60000,
      "dampNs": 3500,
      "brake": true,
      "isTailwheel": false,
      "isSteerable": false,
      "maxSteerRad": 0.0,
      "tireMu": 0.8,
      "corneringStiffnessN": 8000,
      "tire": "legacy-aircam-main",
      "tirePressurePsi": 16.1,
      "toeOutDeg": 2.0,
      "toeOutDegSource": "The matching value for THIS wheel. Toe is per leg (LandingGear.ToeRad reads g.ToeOutDeg and takes the sign from the wheel's own y), so a value on one main and nothing on the other is not 2 degrees out on both -- it is 2 out on one wheel and dead straight on the other, a standing uncancelled side force of about 0.42 of that wheel's load (12 per rad x 2 deg) with nothing opposing it. The 2026-11-10 toe-out commit says \"Sixteen types, both mains each\" and wrote the field to the FIRST main only on fourteen of them; this is the other main on each, with the same number. Ty's knob (LandingGear.ToeOutOverrideDeg) always covered both, which is why the two types he was flying when he chose the number -- the Pitts and the Extra, the two the commit did write both -- felt right."
